---
description: Modification on tiptap editor for template and smart drafting
alwaysApply: false
---

Both editors should generally follow the same logic and reuse shared components (such as export to Word). When modifying the Tiptap toolbar or the page settings/view of one editor, make sure to update the other as well (and possibly the export-to-DOCX settings too).

The main difference lies in the “clause” and “variable” nodes.

In the template editor:
	•	The variable node has an id, a text (the variable’s name, see Smart Drafting variable), and an isLibrary boolean (default: false). This last attribute changes the color for library variables.
	•	The clause node has an id and a text (the clause’s name).

In Smart Drafting:
	•	The variable node has an id (same as in the initial template), a text (the variable’s value, see Smart Drafting sidebar), and a confidence attribute. This confidence value (also updated when modifying the sidebar) determines the color.
	•	The clause node has an id (same as in the initial template), a text (the actual content of the clause or its variant), and an approved attribute. This approved attribute also lead to change of color.

In both cases, the editor is tightly linked to the corresponding sidebar to reflect changes and/or drag-and-drop operations of variables and clauses. Idem for color rules, color in text should correspond to sidebar card as much as possible.

Finally, the template editor includes a read-only mode (allowing users to preview a template from the Nora library before uploading it to their own workspace) where all editing actions are disabled. This mode does not exist in Smart Drafting.